<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Cleartext is off everywhere (`android:usesCleartextTraffic="false"` in the manifest) with
  exactly two exceptions in a **release** build, and both of them are this app talking to
  itself.

  `127.0.0.1` and `localhost` are the **shell's own bundle server** (BRIDGE.md §6): the
  active bundle is served from `http://127.0.0.1:41847`, which is a secure context by
  specification but is still plain HTTP as far as Android's cleartext policy is concerned.
  Without this entry the webview cannot load the app at all. This is not a development
  convenience — it is required in release.

  **Nothing else may be plain HTTP.** The bearer token (SPEC §5.2) rides every manifest,
  bundle and admin-export request, and the login POST carries an email and a password; a
  release build that could reach an http:// server would put all of it on the wire in the
  clear, and an on-path attacker could also forge the bundle manifest — which is the input
  to the updater's directory names and hashes. So production servers are HTTPS (SPEC §8:
  "caddy — automatic HTTPS; secure origin is required"), and that is now guaranteed by the
  platform rather than by the operator.

  The emulator's route to a development host — `10.0.2.2`, how a debug build reaches
  `mise run dev` on a laptop — lives in `src/debug/res/xml/` and `src/profile/res/xml/`,
  which override this file for those build types only. `normalizeServerUrl`
  (shell/login_screen.dart) accepts an explicitly typed `http://` URL, so keeping the
  exemption out of release is what stops a user on a LAN where an RFC1918 address happens
  to resolve from getting a working cleartext login.

  `cleartextTrafficPermitted` is per-domain and does not inherit: every host that needs it
  is listed, and nothing else in the app can fall back to HTTP.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <domain-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true">
        <domain includeSubdomains="false">127.0.0.1</domain>
        <domain includeSubdomains="false">localhost</domain>
    </domain-config>
</network-security-config>
